  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139463,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lymphangiectasia affects the bulbar conjunctiva.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139464,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lymphangiectasia presents with ocular surface irritation {22019374}. Most lesions involve the temporal bulbar conjunctiva. A common comorbidity is pterygium {22019374}. If blood is present in the dilated lymphatics, it is termed lymphangiectasia haemorrhagica {9850253}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139465,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lymphangiectasia accounts for 1% of all conjunctival lesions and 5&ndash;8% of all conjunctival cystic lesions {3301209;6629442;25892928}. In the largest series of conjunctival vascular lesions, lymphangiectasia accounts for 19% of 140&nbsp;cases, with a male predilection {21788081;22019374}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139466,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lymphangiectasia can be induced by IgE-mediated ocular surface allergies, degenerative lesions (especially pterygium and conjunctivochalasis), other inflammatory or local neoplastic diseases, organ transplant rejection, or scarring from ocular trauma, surgery, and radiotherapy {3301209;22019374;24590271;27994801}. There is also association with Fabry disease {28500230} and hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis {31074309;31949094}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139467,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lymphangiectasia is caused by the obstruction of normal lymphatic channels by local perilymphatic scarring or distal obstruction {22019374}. Lymphangiectasia haemorrhagica is caused by abnormal communications between conjunctival lymphatics and the deep conjunctival venous plexus {9850253}, with retrograde flow into the lymphatics.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139468,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139469,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Developmentally normal lymphatic channels are dilated and lined by flattened endothelial cells that stain positive with D2-40. The dilated channels may contain proteinaceous material and lymphocyte clusters. The surrounding stroma may be oedematous, fibrotic, and chronically inflamed {22019374;15810951}. The overlying conjunctival epithelium may show squamous metaplasia and/or keratinization {22019374}. In lymphangiectasia haemorrhagica conjunctivae, blood will be seen in the lumen {9850253}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 979,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 744,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139470,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 744,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 980,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 745,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139471,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 745,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 983,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 748,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139472,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 748,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> dilation of normal lymphatic channels, lined by flattened endothelial cells.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> establishing a cause of the secondary lymphatic dilation (e.g. degenerative, inflammatory, iatrogenic, neoplastic); immunohistochemical expression of CD31 and CD34 and staining with D2-40 in the endothelial cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 981,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 746,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139473,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 746,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 982,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 747,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139474,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 265,
        "headingId": 747,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Asymptomatic lesions can be observed. Symptomatic cases can be locally excised {14516817;9333668;13422475;22019374} with a low risk of recurrence. Other modalities include cryotherapy, diathermy, and argon laser {20126499;5781037;9850253}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]